  {
    "episode_id": 96225,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96225.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 80,
    "turn_text": "Even with an audience fixated on weed policy and school lunch, Grassley endeavors to answer every question. And he does it without a bit of impatience or condescension. And you know what? It works. Therein lies the lesson, I think, for caucus candidates. I told Jerry Crawford about that little thesis statement Grassley opens his town hall meetings with, about the spirit of representative government and the importance of engagement between a representative and his constituents. And of course he agreed. And he argued that in the context of the caucuses, the engagement that's possible here is not only valuable to Iowans and to candidates, but also to the national deliberation over our next president. Being there in Iowa provides a ground level perspective and allows for up close scrutiny on candidates that becomes infinitely more difficult as the campaign",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_04",
